AB  - The tea geometrid (<i>Ectropis grisescens</i>) is a significant pest in Chinese tea plantations. Although <i>Metarhizium anisopliae</i> serves as an environmentally friendly biocontrol agent against <i>E. grisescens</i>, the molecular mechanisms underlying the insect's immune response remain unclear. This study investigates changes in immunity-related genes and metabolites in <i>E. grisescens</i> larvae infected with a virulent strain of <i>M. anisopliae</i> through transcriptome sequencing and metabolome analysis. We identified 2409 differentially expressed genes (DEGs) at 48 h post-infection, with 1611 genes up-regulated. GO analysis revealed that 119 DEGs were significantly enriched in immune-related processes. Additionally, 1860 differentially accumulated metabolites (DAMs) were detected, including 652 up-regulated and 1208 down-regulated metabolites, with 236 significantly enriched in 82 KEGG pathways. These findings indicate the activation of immunity-related and detoxifying enzyme-related genes, providing new insights into the physiological and biochemical responses of insects to biopesticides and potential targets for controlling tea geometrid.
